  "difficulty_reason": "The proof keeps a prescribed word omega fixed while deforming the representation: openness of the power map on loxodromic elements of SL3(R) lets the authors replace the image of one generator so that rho(a)^{-m}rho(a^m)rho(b^n) is an n-th power, which forces rho(omega) to be unchanged along the deformation. A one-parameter deformation of the unstable eigenline of rho(a) is then chosen transverse to the attracting plane, so that the coefficient functional theta_t of that plane changes sign on the two sides of t=0 while the approximating functionals of rho_t(a^p)P_0 converge to theta_t; this pins a parameter t_0 with L_0 contained in rho_{t_0}(a^p)P_0. Both rho_{t_0}(omega^q) and rho_{t_0}(a^p omega^q a^{-p}) then preserve that hyperplane and the latter acts on it as a scalar, so the commutator acts as the identity there and, having determinant one, is unipotent. This is the proposition the paper calls the main step towards its main theorem, and the simultaneous prescription-plus-transversality mechanism in a higher-rank setting is above ordinary PhD qualification problems in Lie theory or geometric group theory.",
